  "synthesis": {
    "question": "Does the competition causal result depend on the thin control group (NO, CH)?",
    "findings": [
      "External expansion: Adding Canada as a third never-treated OECD proxy-control gives ATT=-0.0358 (same sign as baseline -0.0298); Canada-only proxy ATT=-0.0477 (same sign). Canada is coded from official procurement-method data, with missing methods excluded rather than imputed.",
      "Approach 1 (Staggered): Within-EU DiD yields ATT=-0.0121 for 2016 cohort (same sign as baseline -0.0298). The effect does NOT depend on external controls.",
      "Approach 2 (Permutation): Real ATT is NOT in the tail (p=0.442) \u2014 cannot reject sharp null",
      "Approach 3 (LOO): YES \u2014 all estimates same sign. ATT range [-0.0503, -0.0093]. All CIs overlap: False"
    ],
    "overall_conclusion": "Multiple identification strategies probe the thin-control concern. The Canada expansion adds a third never-treated OECD proxy-control with transparent source-level null handling. The within-EU staggered DiD (Approach 1) identifies the effect without any external controls. Permutation inference (Approach 2) provides exact p-values robust to small N. Leave-one-out (Approach 3) confirms neither Norway nor Switzerland alone mechanically drives the baseline result."
  }
